{"text": "dishonest", "etymologies": [{"etymologyHTML": "<p>From Middle English <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"enm\">dishoneste</i> (\u201cdishonourable\u201d), from Old French <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"fro\">deshoneste</i>, from Latin <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"la\">dehonestus</i>. Equivalent to <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"en\">dis-</i> +\u200e <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"en\">honest</i>. Displaced native Old English <i class=\"Latn mention\" lang=\"ang\">uns\u014d\u00fef\u00e6st</i>.\n</p>", "pos": [{"pos": "Adjective", "definitions": [{"definition": "Not honest; shoddy.", "quote": [], "relative": {}}, {"definition": "Interfering with honesty.", "quote": [], "relative": {}}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Dishonourable; shameful; indecent; unchaste; lewd.\n", "quote": [{"citation": "1713, [Alexander] Pope, Windsor-Forest.\u00a0[\u2026], London:  [\u2026]  Bernard Lintott\u00a0[\u2026], \u2192OCLC:", "text": "inglorious triumphs and dishonest scars"}, {"citation": "c. '1560, Thomas North, Archontorologion:", "text": "speake dishonest word"}], "relative": {}}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Dishonoured; disgraced; disfigured.\n", "quote": [{"citation": "1697, Virgil, \u201cThe Sixth Book of the \u00c6neis\u201d, in John Dryden, transl., The Works of Virgil: Containing His Pastorals, Georgics, and \u00c6neis.\u00a0[\u2026], London:  [\u2026]  Jacob Tonson,\u00a0[\u2026], \u2192OCLC:", "text": "Dishonest with lopped arms the youth appears, / Spoiled of his nose and shortened of his ears."}], "relative": {}}]}]}], "pronunciation": [["Mid-Atlantic US", "/d\u026az\u02c8\u0252n\u025bst/"], ["UK", "/d\u026a\u02c8s\u0252n\u026ast/"], ["US", "/d\u026a\u02c8s\u0251n\u026ast/"]]}